<!-- ============ CodeJitsu ============ -->
<section class="section" id="codejitsu" aria-labelledby="codejitsu-title">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="section-header">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 · Case study</p>
            <h2 class="section-header__title" id="codejitsu-title">CodeJitsu: an AI coach that watches BJJ sparring footage</h2>
            <p class="section-header__description">
                Upload a Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu sparring video and get a timestamped match breakdown, a technical grade
                and the drills that fix what went wrong. The same weaknesses then feed AI-drafted class plans and
                partner pairings for the next session.
            </p>
        </header>

        <figure class="figure figure--hero fade-up">
            <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-cover.webp" alt="CodeJitsu launch video frame: &quot;Gemini watches every second&quot; beside the four pipeline steps: Auto-Profiler, Event Logger, Event Verifier, Head Coach" width="1600" height="900" fetchpriority="high" />
        </figure>

        <div class="zen-divider"><span>At a glance</span></div>

        <dl class="kv-panel">
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Solo builder: product, architecture, development and operations</dd>
            <dt>Timeline</dt>
            <dd>Hand-built side project from February 2024; developed with a team of AI agents since March 2026</dd>
            <dt>Company</dt>
            <dd>Free demo and internal R&amp;D tool at <a href="https://tknq.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TKNQ</a>, where I'm CTO</dd>
            <dt>Stack</dt>
            <dd>Gemini 4 Argon on Vertex AI · .NET 10 microservices · React 19 · Supabase PostgreSQL · Google Cloud · Cloudflare</dd>
            <dt>Live</dt>
            <dd><a href="https://thecodejitsu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thecodejitsu.com</a></dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>The problem</h3>
            <p>
                A five-minute roll holds dozens of transitions, scrambles and positions that look alike. Coaches can't
                rewatch every round for every student, so detailed feedback rarely happens. Can a multimodal model act
                as a reliable second set of eyes for an instructor, and turn that feedback into the next class?
            </p>
            <p>
                It's also a hard benchmark for video AI: long footage, fast action, and a half guard that looks almost
                the same as a quarter guard. That's why TKNQ keeps CodeJitsu running as its R&amp;D testbed.
            </p>

            <h3>A four-step agentic pipeline</h3>
            <p>
                The first version analysed a whole video in a single Gemini call, which was too much to get right in
                one pass. I split the job into four focused steps, each with its own reasoning level:
            </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="steps steps--4">
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">I</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Profiler</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Identifies the athlete, their opponent and their style.</p>
                <span class="badge badge--blue">Medium reasoning</span>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">II</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Event Logger</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Logs every technique with a timestamp.</p>
                <span class="badge badge--blue">Medium reasoning</span>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">III</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Verifier</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Re-checks each logged call and flags low-confidence ones for review.</p>
                <span class="badge badge--parch">Low reasoning</span>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">IV</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Head Coach</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Writes the grade, strengths, weaknesses and drills.</p>
                <span class="badge badge--gold">High reasoning</span>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>Design decisions</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>The video goes first in every request</strong>, so Gemini reuses it across all four steps through prefix caching instead of paying to re-read it.</li>
                <li><strong>Analysis runs as a background job</strong> (Hangfire on Postgres), with progress pushed to the browser over SignalR.</li>
                <li><strong>A person stays in the loop.</strong> Instructors can edit every AI output before a student sees it.</li>
                <li><strong>Model choice is a setting, not code.</strong> An admin AI Console sets the model, region, reasoning level and token limit per feature, and every run snapshots its settings. Moving to Gemini 4 Argon showed why: it rejects reasoning budgets, has no explicit context caches and ignores sampling settings, so changing the model name alone would have broken the pipeline.</li>
                <li><strong>Knowing when not to use AI.</strong> Technique search first used Grok live search (dead links), then Gemini with Google Search grounding (40–120 seconds, past the 60-second server limit). A plain YouTube Data API search replaced both: instant, cheap, and only real videos.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid--2 u-mt-6">
            <figure class="figure">
                <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-class-curriculum.webp" alt="AI-drafted 60-minute class plan split into warm-up, techniques, drills, sparring and cool-down" width="1053" height="856" loading="lazy" />
                <figcaption>Weaknesses found in students' videos feed an AI-drafted plan for the next class.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="figure">
                <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-partner-pairs.webp" alt="AI partner pairing: four pairs with the model's written reasoning on belt, experience, weight, height and age" width="1073" height="600" loading="lazy" />
                <figcaption>A matchmaking service pairs training partners by belt, weight and experience, and explains its reasoning.</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>Running a paid AI feature as a free demo</h3>
            <p>Every analysis costs real money, so the controls had to be fair to users and safe for the budget:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>3 analyses per account per rolling 24 hours, adjustable live from the AI Console.</li>
                <li>Failed runs are refunded automatically, and automatic job retries are off. Before that change, one failure could quietly re-run the whole paid pipeline up to ten times.</li>
                <li>The limit is checked before upload, two requests can't both take the last slot, and a double-click never costs two runs.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Built and operated by a team of AI agents</h3>
            <p>
                Since March 2026, CodeJitsu has been developed with Claude Code working as five specialised agents,
                each defined by a role file in the repository. I set direction, answer the product questions and
                review what ships.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="zen-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th scope="col">Agent</th><th scope="col">Responsibility</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Project manager</td><td>Breaks work into tasks, delegates, reviews code, enforces quality checks</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Frontend developer</td><td>React and TypeScript UI, state, English and Polish translations</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Backend developer</td><td>.NET APIs, domain services, EF Core models and migrations</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>DevOps engineer</td><td>Docker, Google Cloud, Nginx, CI/CD and deployments</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>QA tester</td><td>Writes tests, verifies changes, hunts edge cases</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>Operations, with the same agents</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>June 2026:</strong> moved the production stack to a company-billed Google Cloud project with a keyless setup: VM identity, Secret Manager, and Workload Identity Federation for CI.</li>
                <li><strong>July 2026:</strong> recovered from a region-wide Google Cloud capacity outage by moving the VM to another zone from a machine image, and added self-healing container restarts.</li>
                <li><strong>August 2026:</strong> moved to a smaller VM and cut hosting cost roughly in half, from about $24.50 to $12 a month, with memory limits keeping it stable.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="zen-divider"><span>Results</span></div>

        <div class="stats">
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">4</div><div class="stat__label">focused agentic steps, each with a verification pass</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">380</div><div class="stat__label">automated tests across backend and frontend</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">~50%</div><div class="stat__label">lower monthly hosting cost, same behaviour</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">0</div><div class="stat__label">redeploys needed to switch Gemini models</div></div>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-card u-mt-6">
            <h3 class="scroll-card__title">What I learned</h3>
            <div class="scroll-card__body">
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Small steps beat one big prompt.</strong> Focused steps with a verification pass are easier to tune, test and trust.</li>
                    <li><strong>Models change faster than code ships.</strong> Treating the model as a setting turned a breaking migration into a two-minute change.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not every feature needs a model.</strong> Technique search got faster, cheaper and more reliable without AI.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cost controls are part of the feature.</strong> Refunds, the in-progress guard and disabled retries belonged in version one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Agents speed up delivery, not judgment.</strong> Product decisions, reviews and approvals stayed my job, and that's where the quality came from.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card__footer u-pt-5">
            <ul class="badge-list">
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Gemini 4 Argon</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Vertex AI</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Claude Code</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">.NET 10</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Hangfire</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">SignalR</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">React 19</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Supabase</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Compute Engine</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">GitHub Actions</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://thecodejitsu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo @Icons.Svg("arrow-up-right", 14)</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ JRE Clipper ============ -->
<section class="section section--surface" id="jre-clipper" aria-labelledby="jre-title">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="section-header">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 · Case study</p>
            <h2 class="section-header__title" id="jre-title">JRE Clipper: "What would Joe Rogan say?"</h2>
            <p class="section-header__description">
                Type a topic, from jiu-jitsu to bees to aliens, and get the exact moments it came up across the Joe
                Rogan Experience archive. Each result is an embedded YouTube player with clickable timestamps that jump
                straight to the discussion.
            </p>
        </header>

        <dl class="kv-panel">
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Solo builder: data pipeline, search, video processing and payments</dd>
            <dt>Model</dt>
            <dd>Unlimited free search for everyone; premium subscribers get automatically edited compilation videos</dd>
            <dt>Stack</dt>
            <dd>Python · .NET · Node.js · Firebase · Vertex AI embeddings and Vector Search · Cloud Run · Pub/Sub · Cloud Storage · Stripe</dd>
            <dt>Live</dt>
            <dd><a href="https://whatwouldjoerogansay.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">whatwouldjoerogansay.com</a></dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>How it works</h3>
        </div>

        <ol class="steps steps--4">
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">I</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Ingest</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Python scripts collect episode metadata and transcripts, then clean and segment them.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">II</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Embed</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Transcript segments are vectorised with Vertex AI's batch embeddings API.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">III</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Search</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Vertex AI Vector Search finds the most relevant segments for a topic; the results come back as timestamped players.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="step">
                <span class="step__numeral">IV</span>
                <h4 class="step__title">Compile</h4>
                <p class="step__body">Premium requests go through Pub/Sub to a Cloud Run service that cuts and joins the segments with FFmpeg.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>What went wrong, and the fix</h3>
            <p>
                The first compilation videos came out with corrupted segments and the wrong total length. The root
                causes were mine: seek-based cutting that wasn't frame-accurate, a concat filter where the concat
                demuxer was needed, mismatched encoding between segments, and FFmpeg errors that were swallowed instead
                of logged.
            </p>
            <p>
                The fix was to cut segments with trim filters, encode every segment with identical settings, validate
                each segment's video and audio streams and frame count before joining, check codec compatibility before
                concatenating, and validate the final video's streams and duration. Every step now logs FFmpeg's
                errors.
            </p>
            <p>
                The product decision mattered as much as the code. Searches are free and unlimited, using official
                YouTube embeds. Only video generation is paid, and it requires sign-in.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card__footer u-pt-5">
            <ul class="badge-list">
                <li class="badge badge--parch">RAG</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Vector Search</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Batch embeddings</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Event-driven</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">FFmpeg</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Firebase Auth</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Stripe</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://whatwouldjoerogansay.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Search the archive @Icons.Svg("arrow-up-right", 14)</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

